  "app": {
    "title": "MSEABOARD",
    "summary": "The person loads sequence alignments, phylogenetic trees, distance or data matrices, tables and molecular structures as files, by drag and drop or by fetching a structure ID. Each one opens as a panel they can drag and resize on a board. From an alignment they create linked panels: a translation, a sequence logo, per-site statistics, a pairwise distance matrix, a colour overview matrix, or a tree built from it with a substitution model they choose. They link panels to each other, so hovering a site, sequence, matrix cell or leaf highlights the matching item in every linked panel. They search for motifs, reorder sequences, pull out subsets of sequences or subtrees, reroot or prune trees and keep notes. Then they export each panel's data or figure, or save the whole board as a JSON file or a share link.",
    "task": {
      "text": "Explore an alignment together with the trees, distance matrices, statistics and structures derived from it, by linking every view so that one item can be followed across all of them.",
      "verbs": [
        "explore",
        "derive",
        "compare",
        "locate",
        "identify"
      ]
    },
    "data": {
      "text": "Multiple sequence alignments with their phylogenetic trees, pairwise distance or numeric matrices, per-site or tabular statistics and 3D molecular structures, which can all be loaded or derived from each other.",
      "item": "a sequence in an alignment, and the site, tree leaf, matrix cell or structure residue it corresponds to",
      "shapes": [
        "sequence",
        "hierarchy",
        "table",
        "field",
        "notes"
      ]
    },
    "stage": {
      "text": "exploring and analysing an alignment and its tree after sequences are aligned, before writing up",
      "terms": [
        "explore",
        "analyze"
      ]
    },
    "mechanism": {
      "text": "Each dataset is a panel on a draggable, resizable grid. Linked panels share one highlight, so pointing at a site, sequence, cell or leaf in one panel highlights the matching item in the others and scrolls to it. Header buttons create new panels already linked to their source, such as logos, statistics, distance matrices and trees.",
      "views": [
        "sequence track",
        "tree view",
        "heatmap",
        "bar chart",
        "table view",
        "image viewer",
        "text editor",
        "canvas"
      ],
      "interactions": [
        "import",
        "hover for detail",
        "linked highlight",
        "search",
        "sort",
        "multi-select",
        "drag to arrange",
        "toggle view",
        "export",
        "undo",
        "write notes",
        "zoom and pan"
      ],
      "patterns": [
        "linked views",
        "dashboard",
        "overview and detail"
      ]
    },
    "field": {
      "text": "multiple sequence alignment and phylogenetic analysis in evolutionary biology",
      "disciplines": [
        "biology"
      ],
      "terms": [
        "phylogenetics",
        "sequence alignment",
        "bioinformatics"
      ]
    },
    "workflow": {
      "name": "derive and link several views of one dataset to follow items across them",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step": "bring the data in",
          "here": "Coloured buttons upload FASTA alignments, Newick or NHX trees, PHYLIP or tabular matrices, TSV, CSV or TXT data and PDB structures, or fetch a structure by its ID. Files can also be dropped onto the board, or a saved board JSON can be loaded.",
          "parts": [
            "top-bar"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "look over every item at a glance",
          "here": "Each file opens as a panel on a 12-column grid that the person drags and resizes. The alignment shows residues coloured by a chosen scheme, and a colour-matrix heatmap gives an overview of the whole alignment.",
          "parts": [
            "workspace-grid",
            "alignment-panel"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "compute a value from the data",
          "here": "From the alignment header the person creates a translation, a sequence logo of information content in bits, per-site conservation and gap statistics, or a normalized Hamming distance matrix. They can also build a tree with FastME or neighbour joining, choosing a DNA or protein substitution model.",
          "parts": [
            "derived-views",
            "heatmap-panel",
            "tree-builder"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "link the views so that one selection shows in all of them",
          "here": "The link button on two compatible panels joins them. Hovering a site, sequence, matrix cell or tree leaf then highlights and scrolls to the same site, the same pair of sequences or the same residues in every linked panel.",
          "parts": [
            "workspace-grid",
            "heatmap-panel",
            "tree-panel",
            "structure-panel"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "filter to the items that matter",
          "here": "Search finds a site index or motif, with arrows to step through the hits. Sequences can be sorted, reversed or restored to file order. Selection mode pulls chosen sequences or tree leaves into a new subset alignment or subtree panel.",
          "parts": [
            "alignment-panel",
            "tree-panel"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "export the result",
          "here": "Each panel downloads its data as FASTA, Newick, TSV, PHYLIP or PDB, or its figure as PNG or PDF. The whole board saves to JSON or a GitHub Gist share link, with undo and redo available throughout.",
          "parts": [
            "top-bar",
            "alignment-panel",
            "heatmap-panel"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distinctive": [
        "Panels are linked through a table of which panel types can link, and a highlight is translated across types: an alignment column becomes a logo column, a matrix cell becomes a pair of sequences, tree leaves or structure residues.",
        "In codon mode, nucleotide alignments are highlighted and linked in blocks of three sites, and dN/dS (omega) per codon is predicted by an embedded neural network.",
        "Trees are rebuilt in the browser by FastME compiled to WebAssembly, with a choice of substitution model, and can be rerooted, pruned or split into subtrees.",
        "Distance matrices are computed both from sequences (normalized Hamming) and from 3D atom coordinates in a web worker, so a structure can be linked to its matrix."
      ]
    }
  },
